1
00:00:01,660 --> 00:00:06,374
Speaker 1: You were a proponent of deep learning before it gained widespread acceptance.

2
00:00:07,595 --> 00:00:10,379
Speaker 1: What did you see in this field that gave you confidence?

3
00:00:10,399 --> 00:00:16,967
Speaker 1: What was your thinking process like in that first decade of the, I don't know what that's called, two thousands, the aughts?

4
00:00:17,948 --> 00:00:20,712
Speaker 0: Yeah, I can tell you the thing we got wrong and the thing we got right.

5
00:00:21,112 --> 00:00:27,540
Speaker 0: The thing we really got wrong was the importance of, the early importance of unsupervised learning.

6
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:33,910
Speaker 0: So early days of Google Brain, we put a lot of effort into unsupervised learning rather than supervised learning.

7
00:00:34,531 --> 00:00:42,504
Speaker 0: And there was this argument, I think it was around, um, two thousand and five after, you know, NeurIPS at that time called NIPS, but now NeurIPS had ended.

8
00:00:43,226 --> 00:00:48,955
Speaker 0: And Geoff Hinton and I were sitting in the cafeteria outside, you know, the conference, we had lunch, we were just chatting.

9
00:00:48,935 --> 00:00:50,257
Speaker 0: And Jeff pulled up this napkin.

10
00:00:50,297 --> 00:00:52,721
Speaker 0: He started sketching this argument on a napkin.

11
00:00:52,901 --> 00:00:53,843
Speaker 0: It was very compelling.

12
00:00:53,863 --> 00:00:54,764
Speaker 0: I'll repeat it.

13
00:00:56,046 --> 00:00:58,290
Speaker 0: Human brain has about a hundred trillion.

14
00:00:58,350 --> 00:01:00,754
Speaker 0: So there's ten to the fourteen synaptic connections.

15
00:01:01,996 --> 00:01:04,801
Speaker 0: You will live for about ten to nine seconds.

16
00:01:05,361 --> 00:01:06,143
Speaker 0: That's thirty years.

17
00:01:06,203 --> 00:01:09,989
Speaker 0: You actually live for two by ten to nine, maybe three by ten to nine seconds.

18
00:01:10,049 --> 00:01:11,972
Speaker 0: So just let's say ten to nine.

19
00:01:11,952 --> 00:01:25,195
Speaker 0: So if each synaptic connection, each weight in your brain's neural network has just a one-bit parameter, that's ten to the fourteen bits you need to learn in up to ten to nine seconds of your life.

20
00:01:26,076 --> 00:01:33,589
Speaker 0: So via this simple argument, which is a lot of problems, it's very simplified, that's ten to the five bits per second you need to learn in your life.

21
00:01:33,569 --> 00:01:35,651
Speaker 0: And I have a one-year-old daughter.

22
00:01:35,671 --> 00:01:42,118
Speaker 0: I am not pointing out ten to five bits per second of labels to her.

23
00:01:43,319 --> 00:01:49,104
Speaker 0: And I think I'm a very loving parent, but I'm just not going to do that.

24
00:01:49,124 --> 00:01:56,932
Speaker 0: So from this very crude, definitely problematic argument, there's just no way that most of what we know is through supervised learning.

25
00:01:57,633 --> 00:02:02,698
Speaker 0: The way you get so many bits of information is from sucking in images, audio, just experiences in the world.

26
00:02:02,678 --> 00:02:12,030
Speaker 0: And so that argument, and there are a lot of known forces argument we should go into, really convinced me that there's a lot of power to unsupervised learning.

27
00:02:13,713 --> 00:02:16,596
Speaker 0: So that was the part that we actually maybe got wrong.

28
00:02:16,696 --> 00:02:24,967
Speaker 0: I still think unsupervised learning is really important, but in the early days, you know, ten, fifteen years ago, a lot of us thought that was the path forward.

29
00:02:25,488 --> 00:02:29,613
Speaker 1: Oh, so you're saying that that perhaps was the wrong intuition for the time.

30
00:02:29,834 --> 00:02:30,294
Speaker 0: For the time.

31
00:02:30,354 --> 00:02:32,297
Speaker 0: That was the part we got wrong.

32
00:02:32,682 --> 00:02:35,829
Speaker 0: The part we got right was the importance of scale.

33
00:02:35,849 --> 00:02:41,261
Speaker 0: So Adam Coates, another wonderful person, fortunate to have worked with him.

34
00:02:42,343 --> 00:02:51,703
Speaker 0: He was in my group at Stanford at the time, and Adam had run these experiments at Stanford showing that the bigger we train a learning algorithm, the better its performance.

35
00:02:51,683 --> 00:02:59,911
Speaker 0: And it was based on that, there was a graph that Adam generated, you know, where the X axis, Y axis, lines going up into the right.

36
00:02:59,931 --> 00:03:03,935
Speaker 0: So the bigger you make this thing, the better performance accuracy is the vertical axis.

37
00:03:04,556 --> 00:03:15,567
Speaker 0: So it's really based on that chart that Adam generated that it gave me the conviction that we could scale these models way bigger than what we could on a few CPUs, which is what we had at Stanford, that we could get even better results.

38
00:03:15,787 --> 00:03:28,207
Speaker 0: And it was really based on that one figure that Adam generated that gave me the conviction to go with Sebastian Thrun to pitch, starting a project at Google, which became the Google Brain Project.

39
00:03:28,227 --> 00:03:29,810
Speaker 1: Google Brain, you can find Google Brain.

40
00:03:29,950 --> 00:03:39,527
Speaker 1: And there the intuition was, scale will bring performance for the system, so we should chase a larger and larger scale.

41
00:03:39,507 --> 00:03:44,154
Speaker 1: And I think people don't realize how groundbreaking of it.

42
00:03:44,314 --> 00:03:49,803
Speaker 1: It's simple, but it's a groundbreaking idea that bigger data sets will result in better performance.

43
00:03:50,324 --> 00:03:52,627
Speaker 0: It was controversial at the time.

44
00:03:52,908 --> 00:04:05,085
Speaker 0: Some of my well-meaning friends, senior people in the machine learning community, I won't name, but some of whom we know, So my well-meaning friends came and were trying to give me friendly friends like, hey, Andrew, why are you doing this?

45
00:04:05,105 --> 00:04:05,646
Speaker 0: This is crazy.

46
00:04:06,066 --> 00:04:07,328
Speaker 0: It's in the neural network architecture.

47
00:04:07,368 --> 00:04:08,649
Speaker 0: Look at these architectures of building.

48
00:04:09,030 --> 00:04:10,111
Speaker 0: You just want to go for scale?

49
00:04:10,151 --> 00:04:11,553
Speaker 0: This is a bad career move.

50
00:04:11,593 --> 00:04:16,358
Speaker 0: So my well-meaning friends, some of them were trying to talk me out of it.

51
00:04:18,221 --> 00:04:26,592
Speaker 0: But I find that if you want to make a breakthrough, you sometimes have to have conviction and do something before it's popular, since that lets you have a bigger impact.

52
00:04:27,027 --> 00:04:29,631
Speaker 1: Let me ask you just in a small tangent on that topic.

53
00:04:30,272 --> 00:04:46,757
Speaker 1: I find myself arguing with people saying that greater scale, especially in the context of active learning, so very carefully selecting the data set, but growing the scale of the data set is going to lead to even further breakthroughs in deep learning.

54
00:04:46,737 --> 00:04:55,773
Speaker 1: And there's currently pushback at that idea that larger data sets are no longer, so you wanna increase the efficiency of learning.

55
00:04:56,034 --> 00:04:57,737
Speaker 1: You wanna make better learning mechanisms.

56
00:04:58,218 --> 00:05:05,691
Speaker 1: And I personally believe that just bigger data sets will still, with the same learning methods we have now, will result in better performance.

57
00:05:05,671 --> 00:05:12,067
Speaker 1: What's your intuition at this time on this dual side?

58
00:05:12,107 --> 00:05:21,250
Speaker 1: Do we need to come up with better architectures for learning or can we just get bigger, better data sets that will improve performance?

59
00:05:21,803 --> 00:05:24,890
Speaker 0: I think both are important and it's also problem dependent.

60
00:05:25,131 --> 00:05:33,450
Speaker 0: So for a few data sets, we may be approaching Bayes error rate or approaching or surpassing human level performance.

61
00:05:33,470 --> 00:05:38,040
Speaker 0: And then there's that theoretical ceiling that we will never surpass, so Bayes error rate.

62
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:44,870
Speaker 0: But then I think there are plenty of problems where we're still quite far from either human level performance or from Bayes error rate.

63
00:05:45,211 --> 00:05:53,303
Speaker 0: And bigger data sets with neural networks without further algorithm innovation will be sufficient to take us further.

64
00:05:54,666 --> 00:06:04,745
Speaker 0: But on the flip side, if we look at the recent breakthroughs using transforming networks or language models, it was a combination of novel architecture, but also scale had a lot to do with it.

65
00:06:04,885 --> 00:06:07,110
Speaker 0: Look at what happened with GPT-II and BERT.

66
00:06:07,170 --> 00:06:09,736
Speaker 0: I think scale was the large part of the story.

67
00:06:10,498 --> 00:06:24,089
Speaker 1: Yeah, that's not often talked about is the scale of the data set it was trained on and the quality of the data set because there's So it was like redded threads that had, they were uprated highly.

68
00:06:24,109 --> 00:06:30,603
Speaker 1: So there's already some weak supervision on a very large dataset that people don't often talk about, right?

69
00:06:31,465 --> 00:06:37,637
Speaker 0: I find that today we have maturing processes to managing code things like Git, right?

70
00:06:37,657 --> 00:06:38,318
Speaker 0: Version control.

71
00:06:38,878 --> 00:06:41,501
Speaker 0: It took a long time to evolve the good processes.

72
00:06:41,782 --> 00:06:49,090
Speaker 0: I remember when my friends and I were emailing each other C++ files in email, you know, but then we had, was it CVS subversion Git?

73
00:06:49,411 --> 00:06:50,472
Speaker 0: Maybe something else in the future.

74
00:06:51,734 --> 00:06:58,822
Speaker 0: We're very immature in terms of tools of managing data and thinking about the clean data and how to solve very hot, messy data problems.

75
00:06:59,223 --> 00:07:02,206
Speaker 0: I think there's a lot of innovation there to be had still.

76
00:07:03,107 --> 00:07:05,470
Speaker 1: I love the idea that you were versioning through email.

77
00:07:06,260 --> 00:07:07,302
Speaker 0: I'll give you one example.

78
00:07:08,084 --> 00:07:20,532
Speaker 0: When we work with manufacturing companies, it's not at all uncommon for there to be multiple labellists that disagree with each other, right?

79
00:07:20,552 --> 00:07:23,940
Speaker 0: And so we would do the work in visual inspection.

80
00:07:24,741 --> 00:07:28,448
Speaker 0: We will you know, take say a plastic pot and show it to one inspector.

81
00:07:28,508 --> 00:07:34,838
Speaker 0: And the inspector, sometimes very opinionated, they'll go, clearly that's a defect, this scratch, unacceptable, gotta reject this pot.

82
00:07:35,499 --> 00:07:43,472
Speaker 0: Take the same pot to a different inspector, different, very opinionated, clearly this scratch is small, it's fine, don't throw it away, you're gonna make us yours.

83
00:07:43,452 --> 00:07:52,883
Speaker 0: And then sometimes you take the same plastic part, show it to the same inspector in the afternoon, I suppose in the morning, and very often they go in the morning to say, clearly this is okay.

84
00:07:52,963 --> 00:07:56,567
Speaker 0: In the afternoon, equally confident, clearly this is a defect.

85
00:07:56,587 --> 00:08:03,935
Speaker 0: And so what is an AI team supposed to do if sometimes even one person doesn't agree with himself or herself in the span of a day?

86
00:08:04,596 --> 00:08:13,206
Speaker 0: So I think these are the types of very practical, very messy data problems that my teams wrestle with.

87
00:08:13,186 --> 00:08:22,021
Speaker 0: In the case of large consumer internet companies, where you have a billion users, you have a lot of data, you don't worry about it, just take the average, it kind of works.

88
00:08:22,562 --> 00:08:33,861
Speaker 0: But in the case of other industry settings, we don't have big data, it's just a small data, very small data sets, maybe around a hundred defective parts or a hundred examples of a defect.

89
00:08:33,841 --> 00:08:42,818
Speaker 0: If you have only a hundred examples, these little labeling errors, if ten of your hundred labels are wrong, that actually is ten percent of your data set has a big impact.

90
00:08:42,837 --> 00:08:43,799
Speaker 0: So how do you clean this up?

91
00:08:43,839 --> 00:08:44,741
Speaker 0: What are you supposed to do?

92
00:08:45,202 --> 00:08:56,102
Speaker 0: This is an example of the types of things that my teams, this is a landing AI example, are wrestling with to deal with small data, which comes up all the time once you're outside consumer internet.

93
00:08:56,302 --> 00:08:57,224
Speaker 1: Yeah, that's fascinating.

94
00:08:57,244 --> 00:09:01,857
Speaker 1: So then you invest more effort and time in thinking about the actual labeling process.

95
00:09:02,298 --> 00:09:03,320
Speaker 1: What are the labels?

96
00:09:03,822 --> 00:09:09,798
Speaker 1: What are the, how are disagreements resolved and all those kinds of like pragmatic real world problems.

97
00:09:09,938 --> 00:09:11,121
Speaker 1: That's a fascinating space.

98
00:09:11,405 --> 00:09:11,765
Speaker 0: Yeah.

99
00:09:11,786 --> 00:09:25,684
Speaker 0: I find that actually when I'm teaching at Stanford, I increasingly encourage students at Stanford to try to find their own project for the end of term project rather than just downloading someone else's nicely clean data set.

100
00:09:26,225 --> 00:09:38,121
Speaker 0: It's actually much harder if you need to go and define your own problem and find your own data set rather than you go to one of the several good websites, very good websites with clean scoped data sets that you could just work on.

